Write a React 19 custom hook in TypeScript (strict mode) that keeps a value together with its undo/redo history. Export it as

```ts
function useUndoRedo<T>(initial: T): {
  state: T;
  set: (next: T) => void;
  undo: () => void;
  redo: () => void;
  canUndo: boolean;
  canRedo: boolean;
  reset: (next: T) => void;
}
```

Behaviour:
- `state` starts as `initial`, with nothing to undo or redo.
- `set(next)` makes `next` the current state and records the previous state so it can be undone. It also discards everything that could be redone.
- If `next` is equal to the current state by `Object.is`, `set(next)` does nothing at all: no history entry is added and the redo history is kept. Values are compared only by `Object.is`, so a different object with the same contents counts as a change.
- `undo()` goes back one step and makes the undone state available to `redo()`; `redo()` goes forward one step. Each can be repeated through the whole history. Calling `undo()` with nothing to undo, or `redo()` with nothing to redo, does nothing.
- `canUndo` / `canRedo` are `true` exactly when `undo()` / `redo()` would change the state.
- `reset(next)` makes `next` the current state and clears both the undo and the redo history.

Each of these functions is called at most once per event handler, and `T` is never a function type.

Use only `react`. Answer with a single ```tsx code block containing only the module.

Write a React 19 custom hook in TypeScript (strict mode) for a bounded counter. Export it as

```ts
function useCounter(options?: {
  initial?: number;
  min?: number;
  max?: number;
  step?: number;
}): {
  count: number;
  increment: () => void;
  decrement: () => void;
  set: (n: number) => void;
  reset: () => void;
}
```

Defaults: `initial` 0, `min` -Infinity, `max` Infinity, `step` 1. You may assume `min <= max`.

Behaviour:
- `count` starts at `initial` clamped to `[min, max]`.
- `increment()` adds `step`, `decrement()` subtracts `step`, and `set(n)` sets the count to `n`; in every case the result is clamped to `[min, max]`.
- `reset()` sets the count back to `initial` clamped to `[min, max]`.
- Several calls in the same event handler all take effect: calling `increment()` twice in one handler adds `2 * step` (still clamped).
- `increment`, `decrement`, `set` and `reset` keep the same function identity across re-renders as long as the option values do not change, even when the caller passes a new options object literal on every render. (A `React.memo` child that receives one of them must not re-render just because `count` changed.)

Use only `react`. Answer with a single ```tsx code block containing only the module.

Write a React 19 custom hook in TypeScript (strict mode) that holds a set of values as immutable state. Export it as

```ts
function useSet<T>(initial?: Iterable<T>): {
  values: ReadonlySet<T>;
  add: (v: T) => void;
  remove: (v: T) => void;
  toggle: (v: T) => void;
  has: (v: T) => boolean;
  clear: () => void;
}
```

Behaviour:
- `values` starts with the items of `initial` (empty when it is omitted). The hook never modifies the object passed as `initial`.
- `add(v)` adds `v`, `remove(v)` removes `v`, `toggle(v)` adds `v` if it is absent and removes it if it is present, and `clear()` removes everything. The component re-renders with the new contents.
- `has(v)` tells whether `v` is in the `values` of the render it was returned from.
- Every change produces a new `Set` object. A `Set` that was returned as `values` earlier is never modified afterwards, so a component that kept it (for example as a prop) still sees the old contents.
- A call that would change nothing (adding a value that is present, removing one that is absent, clearing an empty set) keeps the very same `values` object.
- Several calls in the same event handler all take effect, e.g. `add(a)` followed by `add(b)` adds both.

Use only `react`. Answer with a single ```tsx code block containing only the module.

Write an accessible modal dialog in TypeScript (strict mode) for React 19 and export it as

```ts
export function Modal(props: {
  open: boolean;
  onClose: () => void;
  title: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}): React.ReactElement | null
```

It is fully controlled: it never closes itself, it only asks the parent via `onClose`.

Markup:
- While `open` is `false`, render nothing.
- While `open` is `true`, render an element with `role="dialog"` and `aria-modal="true"`, containing a heading with the text `title` and `children`. The dialog's `aria-labelledby` is the `id` of that heading. Rendering in place or through a portal into `document.body` are both fine.

Focus management. "Focusable" means matching `a[href], button:not([disabled]), input:not([disabled]), select:not([disabled]), textarea:not([disabled]), [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])`.
- When the dialog opens, focus moves to the first focusable element inside it, in document order. If it contains none, the dialog element itself receives focus.
- While it is open, Tab and Shift+Tab keep focus inside the dialog: Tab on the last focusable element moves to the first, Shift+Tab on the first moves to the last; between those, Tab and Shift+Tab move to the next / previous focusable element as usual.
- Pressing Escape while focus is inside the dialog calls `onClose` once. While the dialog is closed, Escape does nothing.
- When `open` changes from `true` to `false`, focus returns to the element that had focus just before the dialog opened.

You may import `react` and `react-dom`. Answer with a single ```tsx code block containing only the module.

Write an accessible star-rating input in TypeScript (strict mode) for React 19 and export it as

```ts
export function StarRating(props: {
  value: number;
  onChange: (v: number) => void;
  max?: number;
  readOnly?: boolean;
}): React.ReactElement
```

It is controlled: it shows `value` and reports changes through `onChange`; it never changes what it shows on its own.

Markup and ARIA:
- An element with `role="radiogroup"` whose accessible name is `Rating`, and that can take keyboard focus (`tabIndex={0}`).
- Inside it, one element with `role="radio"` per star, `max` stars in total (`max` defaults to `5`), in order. Star n's accessible name is `1 star` for n = 1 and `n stars` otherwise (`2 stars`, `3 stars`, ...).
- Star n has `aria-checked="true"` when n equals `value` and `aria-checked="false"` otherwise (so with `value` 0 no star is checked).
- When `readOnly` is set, every star has `aria-disabled="true"`.

Behaviour (when `readOnly` is not set):
- Clicking star n calls `onChange(n)`.
- While the radiogroup has focus, ArrowRight calls `onChange(value + 1)` and ArrowLeft calls `onChange(value - 1)`, kept within 1..`max`: at the boundary (ArrowRight at `max`, ArrowLeft at 1) `onChange` is not called. From `value` 0, ArrowRight calls `onChange(1)`.

When `readOnly` is set, clicks and keys never call `onChange`.

